VA=[
X(1,'TEARS ON THE BATTLEFIELD',
 S('Arjuna weeping on the chariot, Krishna turning to speak to him','As','light','MADHUSŪDANA SPEAKS'),
 'Sañjaya said: To him, thus overcome with pity, his eyes troubled and full of tears, sinking in despair, Madhusūdana spoke these words.',
 [('kṛpayā āviṣṭam','<b>overcome with pity</b>'),('aśru-pūrṇa','<b>full of tears</b>'),('ākula-īkṣaṇam','<b>with troubled eyes</b>'),('viṣīdantam','<b>sinking in despair</b>'),('madhusūdanaḥ','<b>slayer of Madhu</b>: Kṛṣṇa')],
 'Chapter 1 ended with Arjuna slumped in the chariot. Sañjaya picks up the scene: the great warrior is in tears. Now, for the first time, Kṛṣṇa will speak at length.',
 'Sañjaya again uses the word <i>kṛpā</i>, pity. Commentators note that pity which paralyses is different from compassion that acts. Kṛṣṇa is called Madhusūdana, the slayer of the demon Madhu: the one who removes demons will now remove the demon of confusion from Arjuna’s mind.',
 'Tears are not shameful. Even the strongest people break down. What matters is what happens next: whether we stay sunk, or turn to someone who can help us see.',
 'The teacher <b>begins to speak</b>.'),
X(2,'“WHERE DID THIS WEAKNESS COME FROM?”',
 S('Krishna speaking firmly to a weeping Arjuna','K','storm','WHY NOW?'),
 'The Lord said: Where has this weakness come upon you, Arjuna, at this hour of crisis? It is unworthy of a noble person; it leads neither to heaven nor to honour.',
 [('śrī-bhagavān uvāca','<b>the Lord said</b>'),('kutaḥ','<b>from where?</b>'),('kaśmalam','<b>weakness, gloom</b>'),('viṣame','<b>in a crisis</b>'),('anārya-juṣṭam','<b>unworthy of the noble</b>'),('akīrti-karam','<b>bringing dishonour</b>')],
 'The Gītā now calls Kṛṣṇa <i>śrī bhagavān</i>, the Blessed Lord, for the first time. His first teaching is not philosophy but a sharp question: where did this come from?',
 '<i>Ārya</i> here means noble in conduct, not a race. Kṛṣṇa says Arjuna’s state fits neither this world (honour) nor the next (heaven). Commentators see a skilled teacher: first shake the student out of self-pity, then teach. Śaṅkara’s commentary begins its real argument a few verses later, after Arjuna surrenders.',
 'When we are sinking, a kind friend sometimes needs to be firm. Gentle words can wait; first, stand up.',
 'Kṛṣṇa’s first words <b>are a wake-up call</b>.'),
X(3,'“STAND UP!”',
 S('Krishna on the chariot telling Arjuna to stand up','K','sun','UTTIṢṬHA!'),
 '“Do not yield to unmanliness, Pārtha; it does not become you. Cast off this petty weakness of heart and stand up, scorcher of foes!”',
 [('klaibyam','<b>impotence, unmanliness</b>'),('mā sma gamaḥ','<b>do not go to</b>'),('na upapadyate','<b>does not suit</b>'),('kṣudram hṛdaya-daurbalyam','<b>petty weakness of heart</b>'),('uttiṣṭha','<b>stand up!</b>'),('parantapa','<b>scorcher of foes</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa ends his rebuke with a command. He reminds Arjuna who he is: Pārtha, son of Pṛthā, and Parantapa, the one who burns his enemies.',
 'Swami Vivekananda said that if one reads only this verse, one gains the whole message of the Gītā: <i>klaibyaṃ mā sma gamaḥ</i>, do not yield to weakness. The word <i>kṣudra</i>, petty, is pointed: Kṛṣṇa calls the noble-sounding speech of chapter 1 small at heart.',
 'Many of our weaknesses dress up as good reasons. Kṛṣṇa’s word to all of us: do not give in to weakness; you are stronger than you think.',
 'First: <b>do not yield to weakness</b>.'),
X(4,'“HOW CAN I SHOOT BHĪṢMA?”',
 S('Arjuna pointing helplessly toward Bhishma and Drona','As','sage','THEY ARE MY ELDERS'),
 'Arjuna said: How shall I fight Bhīṣma and Droṇa with arrows in battle, Madhusūdana? They are worthy of worship, O slayer of foes.',
 [('katham','<b>how?</b>'),('bhīṣmam droṇam ca','<b>Bhīṣma and Droṇa</b>'),('saṅkhye','<b>in battle</b>'),('iṣubhiḥ pratiyotsyāmi','<b>shall I fight with arrows</b>'),('pūjā-arhau','<b>worthy of worship</b>')],
 'Arjuna replies. His grief now has names: his grandfather Bhīṣma, who raised him, and Droṇa, his teacher.',
 'In Indian tradition, elders and teachers are honoured almost as gods (“ācārya devo bhava”). To shoot arrows at feet one should touch in reverence feels monstrous. Arjuna plays on the names: Kṛṣṇa slays enemies (arisūdana), but these are not enemies.',
 'Facing those we respect in a conflict is among the hardest things. Respect and opposition can coexist, but it takes maturity to hold both.',
 'How can I fight <b>those I worship</b>?'),
X(5,'“BETTER TO BEG”',
 S('Arjuna holding a begging bowl rather than a crown','As','food','BETTER TO BEG'),
 '“Better to live in this world on alms than to kill these noble teachers. Even if they seek gain, by killing my teachers I would only enjoy pleasures stained with blood.”',
 [('gurūn ahatvā','<b>not killing the elders</b>'),('mahā-anubhāvān','<b>noble, great-souled</b>'),('bhaikṣyam','<b>alms</b>'),('artha-kāmān','<b>seeking gain</b>'),('rudhira-pradigdhān','<b>stained with blood</b>')],
 'Arjuna would rather live as a beggar than kill his teachers. A begging life was forbidden to a kṣatriya, yet he would choose it.',
 'The phrase <i>artha-kāmān</i> is read two ways: “though they are greedy for gain” (admitting Droṇa and Bhīṣma fight for wages) or as describing the pleasures Arjuna himself would win. Either way, he sees any victory as tasting of blood.',
 'Sometimes we prefer to lose everything rather than hurt someone we love. The Gītā respects the feeling and asks us to think it through.',
 'Every pleasure would <b>taste of blood</b>.'),
X(6,'“WE DON’T EVEN KNOW WHICH IS BETTER”',
 S('Arjuna looking at a balance scale with question marks on both pans','As','scale','WIN? LOSE?'),
 '“We do not know which is better for us: that we conquer them, or that they conquer us. The sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra, whom we would not wish to live after killing, stand before us.”',
 [('na ca etat vidmaḥ','<b>we do not know this</b>'),('katarat naḥ garīyaḥ','<b>which is better for us</b>'),('jayema','<b>we should win</b>'),('jayeyuḥ','<b>they should win</b>'),('na jijīviṣāmaḥ','<b>we would not wish to live</b>')],
 'Arjuna’s confidence is gone. He no longer knows whether winning or losing is better.',
 'This verse is the honest heart of his crisis: not cowardice but real moral uncertainty. That honesty makes him ready to learn. A mind that admits “I do not know” is open to teaching.',
 'Admitting “I don’t know” is not weakness. It is the first step to finding out.',
 'Honest doubt: <b>“we do not know”</b>.'),
X(7,'“I AM YOUR STUDENT”',
 S('Arjuna kneeling with folded hands before Krishna','KA','heart','TEACH ME!'),
 '“My nature is struck by the fault of weakness; my mind is confused about dharma. I ask you: tell me for certain what is better. I am your student; I have taken refuge in you. Teach me.”',
 [('kārpaṇya-doṣa','<b>the fault of weakness</b>'),('dharma-sammūḍha-cetāḥ','<b>mind confused about duty</b>'),('śreyaḥ','<b>the true good</b>'),('śiṣyaḥ te aham','<b>I am your student</b>'),('śādhi mām','<b>teach me</b>'),('prapannam','<b>surrendered</b>')],
 'The turning point of the Gītā. In chapter 1 Arjuna argued. Now he admits he does not know, and asks for teaching. A friend becomes a disciple; the charioteer becomes a teacher.',
 '<i>Kārpaṇya</i> literally means miserliness, the state of the <i>kṛpaṇa</i>; the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (3.8.10) calls one who dies without knowing the Imperishable a kṛpaṇa, a poor soul. Śaṅkara’s commentary begins in earnest here, from Arjuna’s surrender. The word <i>prapanna</i>, surrendered, is central for Rāmānuja’s tradition.',
 'Real learning starts when we stop defending ourselves and ask. Everyone needs a teacher, friend or mentor to whom they can say: I am stuck; help me.',
 'Teaching begins <b>when we ask honestly</b>.'),
X(8,'NOTHING WILL CURE THIS GRIEF',
 S('Arjuna turning away from a crown and a heaven of gods','As','crown','NO CURE'),
 '“I see nothing that would drive away this grief that dries up my senses, even if I won an unrivalled, prosperous kingdom on earth, or even lordship over the gods.”',
 [('na hi prapaśyāmi','<b>I do not see</b>'),('śokam ucchoṣaṇam indriyāṇām','<b>grief that withers the senses</b>'),('asapatnam ṛddham rājyam','<b>unrivalled prosperous kingdom</b>'),('surāṇām api ca ādhipatyam','<b>even rule over the gods</b>')],
 'Arjuna explains why he needs a teacher: no outer gain can cure his grief.',
 'An important insight: Arjuna sees that his problem is inside, and no outer prize can fix it. Commentators call this the beginning of discrimination (<i>viveka</i>). One who knows that the world cannot cure inner sorrow is ready for knowledge of the Self.',
 'More money, power or status rarely heals inner pain. Arjuna sees it clearly; so can we.',
 'No outer gain <b>can heal inner grief</b>.'),
X(9,'“I WILL NOT FIGHT”',
 S('Arjuna sitting silent with arms folded','As','bow','I WILL NOT FIGHT'),
 'Sañjaya said: Having spoken thus to Hṛṣīkeśa, Guḍākeśa, scorcher of foes, said to Govinda, “I will not fight,” and fell silent.',
 [('evam uktvā','<b>having spoken thus</b>'),('guḍākeśaḥ','<b>conqueror of sleep</b>: Arjuna'),('na yotsye','<b>I will not fight</b>'),('iti govindam uktvā','<b>saying this to Govinda</b>'),('tūṣṇīm babhūva','<b>became silent</b>')],
 'Sañjaya reports Arjuna’s final word: “I will not fight.” Then silence.',
 'The irony is in the names: Guḍākeśa, conqueror of sleep, is sunk in the sleep of confusion; Parantapa, scorcher of foes, refuses to fight. Some note that Sañjaya is also gently telling Dhṛtarāṣṭra: there is still hope that the war may stop. It will not.',
 'Silence after a hard decision can be the space where a better answer arrives. Arjuna has spoken; now he listens.',
 'He says <b>“I will not fight”</b> and falls silent.'),
X(10,'AS IF SMILING',
 S('Krishna smiling gently between the two armies','K','light','☺'),
 'Then, O Bhārata, between the two armies, Hṛṣīkeśa, as if smiling, spoke these words to him who sat grieving.',
 [('tam uvāca','<b>said to him</b>'),('hṛṣīkeśaḥ','<b>Kṛṣṇa</b>'),('prahasan iva','<b>as if smiling</b>'),('senayoḥ ubhayoḥ madhye','<b>between both armies</b>'),('viṣīdantam','<b>to the one in despair</b>')],
 'The scene is set for the teaching: between the armies, Arjuna silent and grieving, Kṛṣṇa about to speak.',
 'Why smile? Commentators offer several readings: the smile of a teacher who knows the problem can be solved; gentle amusement at a warrior talking wisdom while weeping; or simply warmth, to put the student at ease. The setting matters too: the Gītā is spoken in the middle, between two sides, at the point of greatest tension.',
 'A calm smile in a crisis is not mockery. It says: this can be faced. Sometimes that alone helps.',
 'The teaching begins <b>with a smile</b>.'),
X(11,'THE WISE DO NOT GRIEVE',
 S('Krishna teaching, a circle of the living and the departed glowing above','K','light','NO NEED TO GRIEVE'),
 'The Lord said: You grieve for those who should not be grieved for, yet you speak words of wisdom. The wise grieve neither for the dead nor for the living.',
 [('aśocyān','<b>those not to be grieved for</b>'),('anvaśocaḥ','<b>you have grieved</b>'),('prajñā-vādān','<b>words that sound wise</b>'),('gata-asūn','<b>those whose breath has gone</b>'),('agata-asūn','<b>those still alive</b>'),('paṇḍitāḥ','<b>the wise</b>')],
 'The teaching proper begins. Kṛṣṇa points out a contradiction: Arjuna speaks like a learned man, yet grieves like one who does not know.',
 'Śaṅkara takes this verse as the start of the Gītā’s teaching: grief and delusion come from not knowing the Self. A <i>paṇḍita</i> is defined here not by learning but by freedom from grief. The verse sets the theme for the rest of the chapter: what are we, really?',
 'Quoting wise sayings is easy; living them is another matter. The test of understanding is how we meet loss.',
 'Grief comes from <b>not knowing what we are</b>.'),
X(12,'ALWAYS WERE, ALWAYS WILL BE',
 S('Krishna, Arjuna and the kings shown as small eternal flames above their bodies','K','star','WE ALWAYS ARE'),
 '“Never was there a time when I did not exist, nor you, nor these kings; nor shall any of us ever cease to be hereafter.”',
 [('na tu eva aham jātu na āsam','<b>never was I not</b>'),('na tvam','<b>nor you</b>'),('na ime jana-adhipāḥ','<b>nor these kings</b>'),('na eva na bhaviṣyāmaḥ','<b>nor shall we not be</b>'),('ataḥ param','<b>hereafter</b>')],
 'The first reason not to grieve: no one truly dies. I, you and these kings always were and always will be.',
 'The plural “we” has been read in different ways. For Śaṅkara (Advaita), it is a manner of speaking: the one Self appears as many through different bodies. For Rāmānuja and Madhva, Kṛṣṇa clearly affirms many eternal selves, really distinct from God and each other. Traditions differ; all agree the self is eternal.',
 'If the people we love truly are eternal, then death is a parting, not an ending. That can change how we grieve.',
 'No one <b>ever ceases to be</b>.'),
X(13,'CHILDHOOD, YOUTH, OLD AGE',
 S('A child, a young man and an old man in a row, the same flame above each','P','seed','SAME SELF'),
 '“As the embodied one passes in this body through childhood, youth and old age, so it passes into another body. The steady one is not confused by this.”',
 [('dehinaḥ','<b>of the embodied one</b>'),('kaumāram','<b>childhood</b>'),('yauvanam','<b>youth</b>'),('jarā','<b>old age</b>'),('deha-antara-prāptiḥ','<b>passing to another body</b>'),('dhīraḥ','<b>the steady, wise one</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa gives an everyday picture. We already change bodies within one life: the child’s body is gone, yet “I” remain.',
 'The argument is from experience: the self that witnesses childhood, youth and age is not itself changing in the same way. Death, Kṛṣṇa says, is one more change of the same kind. The word <i>dhīra</i>, steady or wise, appears again and again in this chapter as the goal.',
 'Look at an old photo of yourself as a child. The body has changed completely; something has not. That is what Kṛṣṇa is pointing to.',
 'Death is <b>a change of body, not of Self</b>.'),
X(14,'HOT AND COLD COME AND GO',
 S('A steady person while a hot sun and cold snowflake come and go','Y','sun','THEY PASS'),
 '“Contacts of the senses with their objects, son of Kuntī, give cold and heat, pleasure and pain. They come and go; they are not lasting. Endure them, Bhārata.”',
 [('mātrā-sparśāḥ','<b>sense contacts</b>'),('śīta-uṣṇa-sukha-duḥkha-dāḥ','<b>giving cold, heat, pleasure, pain</b>'),('āgama-apāyinaḥ','<b>coming and going</b>'),('anityāḥ','<b>not lasting</b>'),('titikṣasva','<b>endure them</b>')],
 'The first practical lesson. Every feeling, from heat and cold to joy and sorrow, comes from contact between senses and objects, and every such contact passes.',
 '<i>Titikṣā</i>, forbearance, becomes one of the classic six virtues of Vedānta. It is not suppression or numbness, but the patience that lets feelings pass without being swept away. Śaṅkara defines it as bearing pairs of opposites without anxiety or complaint.',
 'Next time you are angry, cold, hurt or thrilled, remember: this will pass. That one thought gives a little space to choose how to respond.',
 'Feelings come and go: <b>endure, do not drown</b>.'),
X(15,'FIT FOR IMMORTALITY',
 S('A calm person whom heat and cold do not disturb, a glow of immortality around them','Y','light','SAME IN BOTH'),
 '“The wise person whom these do not disturb, O best of men, who is the same in pain and pleasure, is fit for immortality.”',
 [('yam hi na vyathayanti ete','<b>whom these do not disturb</b>'),('puruṣa-ṛṣabha','<b>O best of men</b>'),('sama-duḥkha-sukham','<b>same in pain and pleasure</b>'),('dhīram','<b>steady</b>'),('amṛtatvāya kalpate','<b>is fit for immortality</b>')],
 'The reward of endurance: one who stays steady through pleasure and pain becomes fit for <i>amṛtatva</i>, immortality, which here means liberation.',
 'Evenness (<i>sama</i>) is the Gītā’s great word, culminating in 2.48: “evenness is yoga.” Commentators read “immortality” as freedom from the cycle of birth and death. The path to it begins with something simple: not being thrown by every change.',
 'Equanimity is not coldness. It is a steadiness that lets us feel fully without losing ourselves.',
 'Steady in joy and pain: <b>fit for freedom</b>.'),
X(16,'THE REAL NEVER CEASES',
 S('A steady lamp glowing while shadows pass across the wall','','lamp','SAT · ASAT',NIGHT2),
 '“The unreal has no being; the real never ceases to be. The seers of truth have seen the final truth of both.”',
 [('asataḥ','<b>of the unreal</b>'),('na vidyate bhāvaḥ','<b>there is no being</b>'),('sataḥ','<b>of the real</b>'),('na abhāvaḥ','<b>no non-being</b>'),('tattva-darśibhiḥ','<b>by seers of the truth</b>')],
 'A one-line philosophy. Whatever truly is cannot stop being; whatever can stop being was never fully real.',
 'This verse is a corner-stone of Vedānta, read differently by each school. For Śaṅkara, the changing world is <i>asat</i> in the sense of not ultimately real, and only Brahman-Self is <i>sat</i>. Rāmānuja reads <i>asat</i> as the perishable body and <i>sat</i> as the imperishable self, both real but different in nature.',
 'Ask of anything you hold onto: will this last? What does not last cannot be our final refuge.',
 'What is real <b>never stops being</b>.'),
X(17,'THAT WHICH PERVADES ALL',
 S('A glowing thread running through every bead of a necklace','','light','IMPERISHABLE',SKY,''.join(f'<circle cx="{120+k*16}" cy="140" r="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(6))+'<path d="M110 140 h100" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="3"/>'),
 '“Know that to be indestructible by which all this is pervaded. No one can destroy the imperishable.”',
 [('avināśi tu tat viddhi','<b>know that as indestructible</b>'),('yena sarvam idam tatam','<b>by which all this is pervaded</b>'),('vināśam avyayasya','<b>destruction of the imperishable</b>'),('na kaścit kartum arhati','<b>no one can bring about</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa names the real: that which pervades all this. It cannot be destroyed by anyone.',
 'The word <i>tatam</i>, “spread through”, pictures the Self like thread running through beads, or space filling every pot. The same image returns in 7.7: “all this is strung on me like pearls on a thread.” Schools differ on whether the pervader is the individual self (Rāmānuja) or the one Brahman (Śaṅkara).',
 'Whatever we are at the deepest level cannot be harmed by events. Remembering this can give quiet courage.',
 'The imperishable <b>cannot be destroyed</b>.'),
X(18,'BODIES END',
 S('A glowing figure inside a body outline that is fading','K','death','BODIES END'),
 '“These bodies of the eternal, indestructible, immeasurable embodied one are said to have an end. So fight, Bhārata.”',
 [('antavantaḥ ime dehāḥ','<b>these bodies have an end</b>'),('nityasya','<b>of the eternal</b>'),('anāśinaḥ','<b>indestructible</b>'),('aprameyasya','<b>immeasurable</b>'),('tasmāt yudhyasva','<b>therefore fight</b>')],
 'Bodies end; the one who dwells in them does not. Therefore, Kṛṣṇa concludes, fight.',
 '“So fight” can sound abrupt after metaphysics. Commentators stress it is not a general command to violence; it is Arjuna’s specific duty, now freed of the false grief that blocked it. Śaṅkara says the verse does not enjoin war but removes the obstacle of grief to doing one’s duty.',
 'Clear thinking about what lasts can free us to do hard things that fear and grief were blocking.',
 'Bodies end; <b>so do your duty</b>.'),
X(19,'NEITHER KILLER NOR KILLED',
 S('A glowing Self untouched while a sword passes through an outline','','sword','NA HANYATE'),
 '“One who thinks this (Self) a killer, and one who thinks it is killed: neither of them understands. It does not kill, nor is it killed.”',
 [('yaḥ enam vetti hantāram','<b>who thinks it a killer</b>'),('yaḥ ca enam manyate hatam','<b>who thinks it killed</b>'),('ubhau tau na vijānītaḥ','<b>both do not know</b>'),('na ayam hanti','<b>it does not kill</b>'),('na hanyate','<b>nor is it killed</b>')],
 'Arjuna fears being a killer. Kṛṣṇa answers: the true Self neither kills nor is killed.',
 'This verse closely echoes the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.19). Commentators warn against misusing it: it does not license harm, since bodies and actions still have moral weight. It speaks of the Self’s nature, to dissolve the fear and guilt that cloud Arjuna’s judgement.',
 'Our deepest self is untouched by what happens to the body. That is comfort in loss, not an excuse for harm.',
 'The Self <b>neither kills nor is killed</b>.'),
X(20,'NEVER BORN, NEVER DIES',
 S('A radiant point of light with the words unborn, eternal','','light','AJAḤ NITYAḤ'),
 '“It is never born, nor does it ever die; having been, it will not cease to be. Unborn, eternal, everlasting, ancient, it is not killed when the body is killed.”',
 [('na jāyate mriyate vā','<b>is not born, does not die</b>'),('kadācit','<b>ever</b>'),('ajaḥ nityaḥ','<b>unborn, eternal</b>'),('śāśvataḥ purāṇaḥ','<b>everlasting, ancient</b>'),('na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre','<b>not killed when the body is killed</b>')],
 'The most quoted verse on the Self, in the longer triṣṭubh metre. It is nearly identical to Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.18.',
 'Each word denies a change: no birth, no death, no beginning, no end, no ageing (<i>purāṇa</i>, “ancient yet ever new”). The six changes of all living things, birth, existence, growth, change, decay and death, do not touch the Self. It is often chanted at funerals.',
 'This verse is a comfort in grief: the one we have lost was never only the body we knew.',
 'The Self is <b>unborn and undying</b>.'),
X(21,'HOW COULD IT KILL?',
 S('Krishna asking a question, a glowing Self above','K','light','WHOM? HOW?'),
 '“One who knows it to be indestructible, eternal, unborn and unchanging: how can that person, Pārtha, kill anyone, or cause anyone to be killed?”',
 [('veda','<b>knows</b>'),('avināśinam nityam','<b>indestructible, eternal</b>'),('ajam avyayam','<b>unborn, unchanging</b>'),('katham saḥ puruṣaḥ','<b>how can that person</b>'),('ghātayati hanti kam','<b>cause to kill, or kill whom?</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa concludes this line of thought: one who knows the Self cannot think of himself as a killer in the ultimate sense.',
 'Śaṅkara builds a major argument here: the knower of the Self does not see himself as the doer of any action, so for him the ordinary rules of action no longer bind. He reads this as a pointer to renunciation for the wise. Rāmānuja reads it as freedom from false grief while still acting.',
 'Knowing who we are changes how we act. Not carelessly, but without the heavy “I” that adds fear and guilt.',
 'Knowing the Self <b>dissolves “I killed”</b>.'),
X(22,'NEW CLOTHES',
 S('A person taking off a worn shirt and putting on a new one','P','gift','OLD → NEW'),
 '“As a man casts off worn-out clothes and puts on new ones, so the embodied one casts off worn-out bodies and enters others that are new.”',
 [('vāsāṃsi jīrṇāni','<b>worn-out clothes</b>'),('vihāya','<b>casting off</b>'),('navāni gṛhṇāti','<b>takes new ones</b>'),('śarīrāṇi','<b>bodies</b>'),('dehī','<b>the embodied one</b>')],
 'The best-known picture in the Gītā. We do not mourn an old shirt; the Self moves on to a new body.',
 'This is the Gītā’s clearest statement of rebirth. Commentators note that bodies are not always “old” at death; the image is about the attitude, not age. It frames death as a natural transition rather than an end.',
 'Changing clothes is ordinary. The Gītā invites us to see death with the same calm, as part of a longer journey.',
 'Bodies are <b>clothes the Self changes</b>.'),
X(23,'WEAPONS CANNOT CUT IT',
 S('Sword, fire, water and wind all failing to touch a glowing point','','light','UNTOUCHED',SKY,'<path d="M20 30 l40 30" stroke="#888" stroke-width="6"/>'),
 '“Weapons do not cut it; fire does not burn it; water does not wet it; wind does not dry it.”',
 [('na chindanti śastrāṇi','<b>weapons do not cut it</b>'),('na dahati pāvakaḥ','<b>fire does not burn it</b>'),('na kledayanti āpaḥ','<b>waters do not wet it</b>'),('na śoṣayati mārutaḥ','<b>wind does not dry it</b>')],
 'Four elements that destroy every material thing cannot touch the Self, because it is not made of elements.',
 'The verse is chosen for Arjuna: he stands among weapons, and the Self is beyond them. Commentators note that earth, water, fire and air each act on matter; the Self, like space, is subtler than all. Weapons are made of earth (metal); so all four elements are named.',
 'Whatever harms the body cannot harm what we most deeply are. That can be a steadying thought in danger.',
 'Nothing in nature <b>can touch the Self</b>.'),
X(24,'ETERNAL AND UNMOVING',
 S('A mountain standing unmoved while weather swirls around it','','mountain','STHĀṆU · ACALA'),
 '“It cannot be cut, burnt, wetted or dried. It is eternal, all-pervading, stable, unmoving, everlasting.”',
 [('acchedyaḥ adāhyaḥ','<b>uncuttable, unburnable</b>'),('akledyaḥ aśoṣyaḥ','<b>unwettable, undryable</b>'),('sarva-gataḥ','<b>all-pervading</b>'),('sthāṇuḥ acalaḥ','<b>stable, unmoving</b>'),('sanātanaḥ','<b>everlasting</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa repeats the point with more words for the Self’s nature.',
 'Why repeat? Śaṅkara says the subject is so hard to grasp that the teacher comes at it again and again, from different sides. <i>Sthāṇu</i> means a post or pillar, firm-rooted; <i>sanātana</i>, everlasting, is the same word used for “Sanātana Dharma”.',
 'Hard truths need repetition. Do not worry if something deep does not sink in at once.',
 'The Self is <b>eternal and unmoving</b>.'),
X(25,'BEYOND THE SENSES',
 S('An eye, an ear and a thinking head all unable to grasp a light','K','mind','UNMANIFEST'),
 '“It is called unmanifest, unthinkable, unchanging. So, knowing it to be such, you should not grieve.”',
 [('avyaktaḥ','<b>unmanifest</b>'),('acintyaḥ','<b>unthinkable</b>'),('avikāryaḥ','<b>unchanging</b>'),('ucyate','<b>is called</b>'),('na anuśocitum arhasi','<b>you should not grieve</b>')],
 'The Self cannot be seen, thought of as an object, or changed. Knowing this, grief has no ground.',
 '<i>Avyakta</i>: not perceptible by the senses; <i>acintya</i>: not an object of thought. Commentators note the paradox: we are asked to “know” what cannot be known as an object. Vedānta says it is known as the knower itself, by being it, not by grasping it.',
 'Some truths are not grasped like facts. They are recognised quietly, like realising you are the one who is looking.',
 'The Self is beyond <b>senses and thought</b>.'),
X(26,'EVEN IF YOU THINK OTHERWISE',
 S('Krishna presenting two scrolls, one saying eternal, one saying born and dies','K','book','EVEN THEN...'),
 '“And even if you think it is always born and always dies, even then, mighty-armed one, you should not grieve for it.”',
 [('atha ca enam','<b>and if this</b>'),('nitya-jātam','<b>constantly born</b>'),('nityam vā manyase mṛtam','<b>or you think always dying</b>'),('tathā api tvam','<b>even then you</b>'),('na evam śocitum arhasi','<b>should not grieve thus</b>')],
 'A turn in the argument. Kṛṣṇa says: suppose you do not accept the eternal Self. Even then grief is not justified.',
 'Commentators see Kṛṣṇa answering other schools of his day, such as materialists who held that consciousness arises and dies with the body, or Buddhist views of a momentary self. He meets them on their own ground. It shows the Gītā arguing, not just asserting.',
 'A good argument works for people who do not share your starting point. Kṛṣṇa models that.',
 'Even on the other view, <b>grief does not follow</b>.'),
X(27,'WHAT IS BORN MUST DIE',
 S('A circle of seed, sprout, tree and fallen log returning to soil','','seed','BIRTH ⇄ DEATH'),
 '“For death is certain for one who is born, and birth is certain for one who dies. So you should not grieve over what cannot be avoided.”',
 [('jātasya hi','<b>for one born</b>'),('dhruvaḥ mṛtyuḥ','<b>death is certain</b>'),('dhruvam janma mṛtasya ca','<b>birth is certain for the dead</b>'),('aparihārye arthe','<b>over the unavoidable</b>'),('na śocitum arhasi','<b>you should not grieve</b>')],
 'Death follows birth as night follows day. Grieving over the certain is pointless.',
 'The verse is one of the most quoted at funerals. Note the second half: “birth is certain for the dead”, a hint of rebirth even within the “other view”. The argument is down-to-earth: what cannot be avoided must be accepted.',
 'Acceptance is not giving up. It frees energy from fighting the inevitable for what we can still do.',
 'Do not grieve <b>for the unavoidable</b>.'),
X(28,'UNSEEN BEFORE, UNSEEN AFTER',
 S('A line from darkness through a bright middle into darkness again','','light','? → ● → ?',SKY,'<path d="M110 130 h100" stroke="#111" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>'),
 '“Beings are unmanifest in their beginning, manifest in their middle, and unmanifest again at their end, Bhārata. What is there to lament in this?”',
 [('avyakta-ādīni','<b>unmanifest at the beginning</b>'),('bhūtāni','<b>beings</b>'),('vyakta-madhyāni','<b>manifest in the middle</b>'),('avyakta-nidhanāni eva','<b>unmanifest at the end</b>'),('tatra kā paridevanā','<b>what lament is there?</b>')],
 'We came from the unseen and will return to the unseen. Only the middle is visible.',
 'A gentle, almost scientific picture: before birth we were not visible; after death we are not visible. The visible span is a short bright stretch. Commentators compare it to a dream, or to waves rising and falling back into the sea.',
 'Seen against the vast before and after, our quarrels and fears look smaller. That perspective can bring peace.',
 'Life is a bright stretch <b>between two unknowns</b>.'),
X(29,'A WONDER',
 S('People looking with wonder at a glowing figure, some speaking, some listening','P','star','AŚCARYAVAT!'),
 '“Someone sees it as a wonder; another speaks of it as a wonder; another hears of it as a wonder; yet even having heard, no one truly knows it.”',
 [('āścaryavat','<b>as a wonder</b>'),('paśyati kaścit','<b>someone sees</b>'),('vadati','<b>speaks</b>'),('śṛṇoti','<b>hears</b>'),('na ca eva kaścit','<b>yet no one</b>')],
 'The Self is so marvellous that seeing, speaking and hearing of it are all wonders, yet even hearing is not enough.',
 'This echoes the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.7): the teacher is wonderful, the learner rare. Śaṅkara reads “no one knows it” as “hardly anyone”: knowledge of the Self is rare. The verse warns against thinking that hearing about it is the same as realising it.',
 'Reading about the Self is a start, not the goal. Wonder is the right attitude: openness rather than the feeling that we already know.',
 'The Self is <b>a wonder</b>, rarely truly known.'),
X(30,'IN EVERY BODY',
 S('Many different bodies, each with the same small light inside','','family','IN ALL',SKY,''.join(GLOW(122+k*24,120,6,'#ffd60a',.95) for k in range(4))),
 '“The embodied one in everyone’s body is eternally unkillable, Bhārata. So you should not grieve for any being.”',
 [('dehī nityam avadhyaḥ','<b>the embodied one is ever unkillable</b>'),('ayam dehe sarvasya','<b>in everyone’s body</b>'),('tasmāt sarvāṇi bhūtāni','<b>therefore for all beings</b>'),('na tvam śocitum arhasi','<b>you should not grieve</b>')],
 'The section on the Self closes: what is in every body cannot be killed; so do not grieve for anyone.',
 '“In everyone’s body”: the same truth applies to Bhīṣma, Droṇa, and every soldier. This is the first hint of a theme the Gītā develops later: seeing the same Self in all beings (6.29, 13.27).',
 'Seeing the same life in every being changes how we treat others. It is the root of real compassion.',
 'The same Self <b>is in every body</b>.'),
X(31,'YOUR OWN DHARMA',
 S('Arjuna as a protector, shielding a crowd','A','armyb','PROTECT'),
 '“Looking at your own dharma too, you should not waver. For a warrior there is nothing better than a righteous war.”',
 [('sva-dharmam','<b>one’s own duty</b>'),('avekṣya','<b>considering</b>'),('na vikampitum arhasi','<b>you should not tremble</b>'),('dharmyāt yuddhāt','<b>than a righteous war</b>'),('kṣatriyasya','<b>for a warrior</b>')],
 'A second kind of argument begins: from duty. Arjuna is a kṣatriya, a protector; refusing a just war is abandoning those he is sworn to protect.',
 'The Gītā argues on several levels. Having spoken of the eternal Self, Kṛṣṇa turns to <i>svadharma</i>, one’s own duty in society. A war is <i>dharmya</i>, righteous, when it is fought to protect, after peace has been tried. Kṛṣṇa himself had gone to the Kauravas as a peace envoy before the war.',
 'Each of us has roles: parent, worker, citizen. Running from the duties of our role, however hard, lets down those who depend on us.',
 'Do <b>your own duty</b>; do not run from it.'),
X(32,'AN OPEN DOOR',
 S('A golden door to heaven standing open','A','light','AN OPEN DOOR'),
 '“Happy are the warriors, Pārtha, who meet such a war that comes unsought, like an open door to heaven.”',
 [('yadṛcchayā','<b>by chance, unsought</b>'),('upapannam','<b>come about</b>'),('svarga-dvāram apāvṛtam','<b>an open door to heaven</b>'),('sukhinaḥ kṣatriyāḥ','<b>happy are the warriors</b>'),('labhante','<b>who obtain</b>')],
 'From the warrior’s code: a just war that comes without one’s seeking is a chance for honour and heaven.',
 'This is the lowest rung of Kṛṣṇa’s arguments: the code of a warrior culture. He meets Arjuna where he stands before lifting him higher. Commentators note the word <i>yadṛcchayā</i>: the Pāṇḍavas did not seek this war; they had asked for five villages.',
 'A hard situation we did not seek can be a chance to rise. How we meet it is ours to choose.',
 'An unsought, just fight: <b>an open door</b>.'),
X(33,'IF YOU REFUSE',
 S('Arjuna walking away from his bow, a broken medal on the ground','As','bow','DUTY DROPPED'),
 '“But if you will not fight this righteous war, then, giving up your own dharma and your honour, you will incur sin.”',
 [('atha cet','<b>but if</b>'),('tvam imam dharmyam saṅgrāmam','<b>you this righteous battle</b>'),('na kariṣyasi','<b>will not do</b>'),('tataḥ sva-dharmam kīrtim ca hitvā','<b>then giving up duty and honour</b>'),('pāpam avāpsyasi','<b>you will incur sin</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa turns Arjuna’s argument around. Arjuna feared sin in fighting; Kṛṣṇa says the sin is in refusing.',
 'Here “sin” means abandoning one’s duty, especially to the innocent who depend on one’s protection. Commentators link this to 3.35: one’s own dharma, even imperfect, is better than abandoning it.',
 'Not acting is also a choice with consequences. Walking away from a duty can harm others as much as a wrong act.',
 'Refusing duty <b>is also a sin</b>.'),
X(34,'LASTING DISHONOUR',
 S('A crowd pointing and whispering','As','family','"HE RAN!"'),
 '“People will speak of your dishonour for ever. And for one who has been honoured, dishonour is worse than death.”',
 [('akīrtim ca api bhūtāni','<b>and people will (speak of) dishonour</b>'),('kathayiṣyanti te avyayām','<b>will tell of yours, lasting</b>'),('sambhāvitasya','<b>for one honoured</b>'),('akīrtiḥ','<b>dishonour</b>'),('maraṇāt atiricyate','<b>exceeds death</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa speaks the language of honour, which mattered greatly to a warrior like Arjuna.',
 'Again this is a worldly argument, deliberately used. Some modern readers find appeals to reputation shallow; commentators answer that a good name is the trust others place in us, and losing it through cowardice harms them too.',
 'Reputation is not everything, but trust built over years is precious. Living up to the trust others place in us matters.',
 'Dishonour <b>is worse than death</b> to the honoured.'),
X(35,'“THEY WILL SAY YOU WERE AFRAID”',
 S('Kaurava warriors laughing and pointing','','army','"FEAR!"'),
 '“The great warriors will think you fled the battle out of fear; and those who held you in high esteem will think little of you.”',
 [('bhayāt raṇāt uparatam','<b>withdrawn from battle out of fear</b>'),('maṃsyante tvām mahārathāḥ','<b>the great warriors will think you</b>'),('yeṣām ca tvam bahu-mataḥ','<b>those who esteemed you</b>'),('bhūtvā yāsyasi lāghavam','<b>you will fall in their eyes</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa points out how Arjuna’s refusal will be read: not as compassion, but as fear.',
 'This is a sharp psychological point. Others cannot see inside us; they see the act. Arjuna’s pity, however sincere, will look like cowardice to Karṇa, Duryodhana and the rest. Kṛṣṇa makes him face how his choice will look.',
 'Our motives may be pure, but others judge by actions. It is worth asking how a decision will be understood, not only how it feels.',
 'Pity will <b>look like fear</b>.'),
X(36,'WHAT COULD BE MORE PAINFUL?',
 S('Enemies jeering at a downcast Arjuna','As','army','TAUNTS'),
 '“Your enemies will say many unspeakable things, mocking your strength. What could be more painful than that?”',
 [('avācya-vādān','<b>unspeakable words</b>'),('bahūn vadiṣyanti','<b>will say many</b>'),('tava ahitāḥ','<b>your enemies</b>'),('nindantaḥ tava sāmarthyam','<b>mocking your ability</b>'),('tataḥ duḥkhataram nu kim','<b>what is more painful?</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa completes the warrior’s argument: mockery from enemies would be the greatest pain.',
 'Verses 2.32–37 form a unit, often called the argument from the world. Commentators observe that Kṛṣṇa uses every means: the eternal Self (2.11–30), duty (2.31), honour (2.32–37). A good teacher speaks to the whole person.',
 'Being mocked hurts, but it should not be our main reason to act. Kṛṣṇa will soon offer a far better reason.',
 'The pain of <b>an enemy’s mockery</b>.'),
]
